Apology, Crito, and Phaedo of Socrates (@socratic-dialogues: 0, is #book, #part-of @plato, #contains @apology, #contains @crito, #contains @phaedo) { r_00000000000000000000001005
Plato. Translated by Henry Cary, with an introduction by Edward Brooks. Project Gutenberg ebook 13726. English. [Original book and publication notices](https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/13726). The retained EPUB preserves the original text and notices; Project Gutenberg identifies this edition as public domain in the USA.

Read the dialogue before using the introduction as an interpretation. The three texts share the setting of Socrates' trial, imprisonment, and death, but develop distinct questions and arguments.

| Text | Guided reading |
| --- | --- |
| [Apology](Apology-Cary.lingua#r_00000000000000000000001006) | [Why examine a life?](../socratic/Apology.lingua#r_0DWAPFJNVYT6WNTRT46ZVK82X4) |
| [Crito](Crito-Cary.lingua#r_00000000000000000000001007) | [Law, conscience, and action](../socratic/Crito.lingua#r_54N10WGKCSCKW6XM202PCG7ZDH) |
| [Phaedo](Phaedo-Cary.lingua#r_00000000000000000000001008) | [Soul, death, and the examination of a proof](../plato/Phaedo.lingua#r_6JFZZP5W43FY7FFQENQNH96AFQ) |

[Jowett's volume II](Dialogues-Jowett.lingua#r_1AD7N0E0ZYZ07XSMQWSK5PA5X8) provides a comparison translation and adds Euthyphro, Meno, and Gorgias. The [Ancient Greek philosophy study](../Ancient-Greek-Philosophy.lingua#r_464ZHR7ZYC234A0XX2QEW47VB3) explains the first sequence and the continuations.

**Return:** [Plato](../plato/Plato.lingua#r_00000000000000000000001004) · [Philosophy](../../Philosophy.lingua#r_00000000000000000000001001).
} r_00000000000000000000001005

